Source: viewpie
Section: video
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Rules-Requires-Root: no
# cargo and rustc are not listed: the toolchain comes from rustup, which
# dpkg cannot see, so the build passes -d. It never reaches the package --
# the binary links only the C libraries below.
# No libdrm-dev: the `drm` crate speaks to the kernel by ioctl and links no C
# library, and src/wrapper.h deliberately leaves libdrm's headers out, so the
# binary needs only libav. Verified with objdump -p: libavcodec, libavformat,
# libavutil, libgcc_s and libc, on both architectures.
#
# clang is what bindgen loads libclang through; without it the build fails in
# build.rs rather than at link time.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               pkg-config,
               libavcodec-dev,
               libavformat-dev,
               libavutil-dev,
               libclang-dev,
               clang

Package: viewpie
# any, not a list: the package is built for whatever architecture it is built
# on, and restricting it means dh silently does nothing when the build host is
# not on the list.
Architecture: any
# Derived from what the binary links rather than listed by hand: the set is
# large, because libavcodec pulls in every codec it can decode, and a hand
# written list goes stale the first time something new is linked.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         adduser
Description: Camera wall for Raspberry Pi, on one atomic KMS commit
 Viewpie decodes RTSP camera feeds with ffmpeg and scans them out on KMS
 overlay planes, one plane per viewport, with no X11, Wayland, desktop
 environment or compositor.
 .
 Every viewport is put on screen by a single atomic commit, so each advances on
 the same vsync rather than sharing a page-flip budget. A viewport's crop is a
 plane property, not a filter, so trimming a seam costs nothing. Frames that
 a hardware decoder produced are imported by their dmabuf and never copied,
 including the column-tiled format a Raspberry Pi's HEVC block emits.
